---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/computer-science/expert/cryptography">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Cryptography is <strong>mathematics as a trust machine</strong>: making secrets provably hard
    to read, signatures impossible to forge, and tampering detectable — without trusting the
    channel, the carrier, or even the other party's honesty. Modern crypto rests on
    <em>hardness assumptions</em>: problems (factoring, discrete logs) believed intractable. When
    the assumptions shift (quantum computers), the crypto must too.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the five ideas between you and the padlock icon" widget="equationStep" config={cryptoCfg} />

  <h2>The rules experts live by</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Don't invent your own crypto.</strong> Home-made schemes fall to techniques you've never heard of. Use audited libraries and standard constructions — this is the first and last rule.</li>
    <li><strong>Kerckhoffs's principle (1883):</strong> assume the attacker knows the system; security must live in the key alone. "Security through obscurity" is not security.</li>
    <li><strong>End-to-end beats transport:</strong> TLS protects messages between servers; end-to-end encryption (Signal protocol) means even the operator can't read them. Different trust models for different needs.</li>
    <li><strong>The quantum horizon:</strong> Shor's algorithm (1994) would break RSA and Diffie–Hellman on a large quantum computer. The migration to post-quantum algorithms (NIST standards, 2024) is underway — the hardest deadline in security is the one you can't verify yet ("harvest now, decrypt later").</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Diffie & Hellman (1976); Rivest, Shamir & Adleman (1978); NIST Post-Quantum Cryptography standards (2024); Kerckhoffs (1883).</p>
</TopicPage>
